**Short answer**

```js
// Input array (any length)
const arr = [3, 1, 2, 1, 4, 3];

// 1️⃣ Remove duplicates → Set  
// 2️⃣ Convert back to array → [...set]  
// 3️⃣ Sort numerically ascending  

const uniqSorted = Array.from(new Set(arr)).sort((a,b)=>a-b);

console.log(uniqSorted);   // ➜ [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

---

### Why this works

| Step | What it does | Time complexity |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| `new Set(arr)` | Keeps only one copy of each value – automatically removes duplicates. | **O(n)** |
| `[...set]` / `Array.from(set)` | Turns the Set back into a plain array so we can call `.sort()`. | **O(k)** where *k* ≤ *n* |
| `.sort((a,b) => a - b)` | Numeric comparison sorts values in ascending numerical order. Default lexicographic sort would give wrong results (`[10, 2]`). | **O(k log k)** |

Overall time: **O(n + k log k)** ≈ **O(n log n)** when all items are distinct. Space usage: **O(k)** extra memory for the Set/array.

---

### Alternative implementations

#### In Python

```python
arr = [3, 1, 2, 1, 4, 3]
result = sorted(set(arr))
print(result)          # -> [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

#### Using functional style (JavaScript)

If you prefer chaining:

```js
const result = [...new Set([3, 1, 2, 1, 4, 3])].sort((x,y)=>x-y);
```

#### Without built‑in Set (manual deduplication)

For environments without ES2015 support:

```js
function uniqSort(array){
    const seen = {};
    const out = [];
    for(const v of array){
        if(!seen[v]){
            seen[v]=true;
            out.push(v);
        }
    }
    return out.sort((a,b)=>a-b);
}
```

This uses an object as a hash map to track already‑encountered values.

---

### Edge cases & notes

| Case | Result | Explanation |
|------|--------|-------------|
| Empty array | `[]` | No elements to process. |
| All identical | Single element | Duplicates removed, leaving just one instance. |
| Already sorted unique | Same array | Sorting step keeps same order. |
| Large integers | Works fine | Numerical comparator handles big ints correctly. |
| Non‑numeric types | Comparator may fail | Ensure data is numeric before calling `(a,b)=>a-b`. |

---

### Summary

Use a `Set` (or equivalent hash structure) to drop duplicates, then sort numerically. That gives you a clean, efficient solution that runs in roughly \(O(n \log n)\) time and requires linear auxiliary space.
